/* The two-panel shell's own rules, appended by the port's sfduo-shell-css
   after the port's (/usr/share/sfduo/gtk.css.in) while item-shell is
   installed. The same tokens (@W@, @LEFT@, @RIGHT@, @OFFGRID@...) are filled
   in with the same numbers. */

/* ---- the bar ---------------------------------------------------------- */

/* One window on one panel (#79, patches/phosh/0006): that half's bar folds
   away to a handle and the free half's bar says all of it - signal, clock,
   indicators - so the window has its panel's whole height. The contents
   cross by fading, out on one half and in on the other; the fold itself is
   the compositor sliding the surface. The handle left on the taken half is
   transparent, not a black line over the window's edge. */
#top-bar > * { transition: opacity 250ms ease-out; }
.phosh-bar-all #top-bar > :first-child,
.phosh-bar-all #top-bar > label,
.phosh-bar-all #top-bar > :last-child { opacity: 1; }
.phosh-bar-none #top-bar > :first-child,
.phosh-bar-none #top-bar > label,
.phosh-bar-none #top-bar > :last-child { opacity: 0; }
.phosh-bar-none #top-bar-bin { background-color: transparent; }

/* ---- the desktop ------------------------------------------------------ */

/* Phosh has no home screen. With nothing running it shows the app grid, and
   there is no state in which the device shows a wallpaper and nothing else -
   which is what this device wants, since its launcher is the dock along the
   bottom. So the grid is pushed past the bottom edge: it is still there, still
   reachable by search when the margin is removed, but nothing of it is on the
   screen and nothing of it can be touched. @OFFGRID@ is twice the longer
   side, more than the screen in either orientation.

   The alternative was to cover the grid with the dock, which worked and cost
   the notification shade: pulled down, it unfolded underneath the cover and
   could not be reached. */
phosh-app-grid { margin-top: @OFFGRID@px; }
